# aether_pair_v2_sympathy — analytic-signal pair convention.
#
# Same three-voice A / C# / E topology as aether_pair_sympathy.aither
# but the (dielectric, magnetic) pair is sourced from analytic(strike)
# instead of (strike, strike-prevStrike). The dielectric carries the
# strike's real part; the magnetic carries its Hilbert transform.
# Both components have identical magnitude spectra at every frequency
# — they differ only by 90° phase shift. This is the textbook-correct
# realisation of "fixed quadrature" that Steinmetz's framing
# describes.
#
# ============================================================ VERDICT
#
# PARTIAL POSITIVE — but a different shape than derivative-pair.
#
# Test 3 (reinforce/cancel) PASSES identically — 209 dB span,
# substrate confirmed sound. Selective-listening between writers
# also confirmed. But Test 2 (rotation as continuous spectral morph)
# is FLATTER under analytic-pair than under derivative-pair: centroid
# range collapses from 1.91× (derivative) to 1.13× (analytic, ≈
# scalar's 1.15×). The diagnosis is structural and clean:
#
#   The analytic pair is two equal-magnitude components in true 90°
#   phase quadrature. Rotation in that plane is a true phase shift
#   on the underlying signal — re*cos(θ) - im*sin(θ) is just the
#   same content with phase added. A high-Q resonator (receiver
#   voice) is invariant to phase shifts: its impulse response selects
#   frequency content and is insensitive to absolute input phase.
#   So rotation produces no audible amplitude/spectral change.
#
# Derivative-pair's centroid variation (1.91×) was an *artefact* of
# the convention's built-in spectral asymmetry (mag is high-pass-
# attenuated re), not a music-theoretic feature. Now that the
# spectral asymmetry is removed by switching to analytic, the
# rotation knob's audible effect collapses to scalar baseline.
#
# THE THREE TESTS, with audit numbers
#
# Test 1 — phase-coherent sympathetic resonance (PASSES literal)
#   Tool: tests/phase_histogram.nim, 50 sec render, 99 strikes
#
#   patches/aether_pair_v2_test1_pair.aither (rotation = π/2):
#     mean angle = 0.503 π   σ = 0.203 π   verdict: σ < π/4 (peaked)
#
#   For comparison:
#     derivative-pair:   σ = 0.130 π   mean = 0.730 π
#     scalar control:    σ = 0.157 π   mean = 0.198 π
#
#   All three peaked — the test's design doesn't differentiate
#   architectures (periodic strikes give deterministic responses).
#   Analytic-pair's mean angle sits at exactly π/2 — the Hilbert
#   quadrature signature. The σ is wider than derivative-pair (less
#   peaked) but still well under π/4.
#
# Test 2 — rotational morph as continuous live knob (FAIL — flatter than baseline)
#   Tool: tests/temporal_audit.nim, 30 sec, 1.0 sec windows,
#         rotation phasor 1/30 Hz
#
#   patches/aether_pair_v2_test2_pair.aither:
#     centroid mean = 286.3 Hz   σ = 10.0 Hz   range = 266 → 301 Hz   (1.13×)
#     RMS mean      = -37.79 dB  σ =  1.76 dB  (RMS modulates ~6 dB)
#
#   For comparison:
#     derivative-pair: centroid σ = 51.1 Hz   range 1.91×   RMS σ = 7.12 dB
#     scalar control:  centroid σ =  9.9 Hz   range 1.15×   RMS σ = 7.59 dB
#
#   Analytic-pair's centroid stddev is 5× SMALLER than derivative-
#   pair's, essentially identical to scalar. The literal 2× criterion
#   fails (1.13×) — and fails harder than derivative-pair did. RMS
#   modulation across rotation is only ~6 dB peak-to-peak (vs 30 dB
#   for derivative).
#
#   The diagnosis: analytic-pair rotation = pure phase shift = no
#   audible effect through a high-Q bandpass.
#
# Test 3 — in-phase reinforces, out-of-phase cancels (PASSES — same as derivative)
#   patches/aether_pair_v2_test3_inphase.aither   (theta=0):    RMS = -31.0 dB
#   patches/aether_pair_v2_test3_outofphase.aither (theta=π):   RMS = -240.0 dB
#   patches/aether_pair_test3_scalar.aither (reused):           RMS = -31.0 dB
#
#   209 dB span — far exceeds the 6 dB criterion. Reinforce/cancel
#   is convention-independent (it's signed additive arithmetic, which
#   any pair representation supports identically).
#
# WHAT THE ANALYTIC-PAIR RESULT REVEALS
#
# Tests 2's result is informative beyond a simple pass/fail. The
# derivative-pair's centroid morph was a side-effect of having the
# magnetic component carry a different magnitude spectrum than the
# dielectric (high-pass-like attenuation at audio frequencies).
# That spectral asymmetry made rotation act as a magnetic-attenuator
# with audible amplitude/timbre variation. The analytic convention
# eliminates this asymmetry — both components carry the same audio
# magnitude — and rotation collapses to phase-shift-only, which a
# high-Q bandpass doesn't hear.
#
# So neither convention delivers the "rotation as a continuous live
# knob" payoff cleanly. Derivative-pair has audible variation but
# spiky and convention-driven. Analytic-pair has smooth-but-tiny
# variation that the receiver bandpass filters out. The pair-valued
# substrate's *real* value lies elsewhere:
#
#   1. Test 3's reinforce/cancel — controllable angle-based
#      cancellation, robust across both conventions.
#   2. Selective listening between multiple writers — V3 reading
#      at φ matches V2's write angle θ isolates V2 from V1.
#      Verified: with V1 writing pure dielectric and V2 writing
#      analytic-pair rotated by π/2, V3 at φ=π/2 reads only V2's
#      contribution; at φ=0 reads only V1.
#   3. Likely (option B): cybernetic-loop state-bearing — the
#      magnetic component IS state, available as a 2D phase-space
#      coordinate the scalar version doesn't provide. Untested.
#
# WHAT'S COMMITTED
#
# - This patch (aether_pair_v2_sympathy.aither) — the canonical
#   analytic-signal sympathy patch with full audit numbers.
# - Four supporting test patches (aether_pair_v2_test1_pair.aither,
#   aether_pair_v2_test2_pair.aither, aether_pair_v2_test3_{inphase,
#   outofphase}.aither). Scalar controls reuse the v1 patches.
# - pole.md "Pair-valued aether: analytic-signal convention is
#   structurally cleaner but rotation collapses" section.
# - aether.md Gap 3 updated.
#
# WHAT'S *NOT* CLAIMED
#
# - The analytic-pair is not promoted to canonical. The substrate
#   works structurally; rotation as a music-theoretic knob does not.
# - No new primitive, no engine change.
# - The integral-pair convention (mag = running sum of strike)
#   exists as a third candidate per pole.md but is NOT tested in
#   this commit.
# - Option B (testing pair-valued on cybernetic_muqabala) follows
#   later, informed by but not gated on this result.

$prevStrike = 0.0   # legacy slot kept for compatibility — unused here

let driveAmp      = 10000000.0
let burstAmp      = 1000000.0
let burstDecaySec = 0.03
let aetherWrite   = 0.1
let aetherDecay   = 0.95
let coupling      = 1.0
let v2v3Couple    = 1.0
let damping       = 0.001
let magScale      = 1.0
let rotationAngle = 0.0
let strikeRate    = 2.0

$aetherDie = 0.0
$aetherMag = 0.0

play coupled:
  let trig    = impulse(strikeRate)
  let burst   = pluck(trig, burstDecaySec)
  let strike  = trig * driveAmp + noise() * burst * burstAmp

  # Analytic-pair drives — equal-magnitude components in 90° quadrature
  let strikePair = analytic(strike)
  let dieDrive   = strikePair[0]
  let magDrive   = strikePair[1]

  $aetherDie = $aetherDie * aetherDecay + dieDrive * aetherWrite
  $aetherMag = $aetherMag * aetherDecay + magDrive * aetherWrite * magScale

  let pair  = rotate($aetherDie, $aetherMag, rotationAngle)
  let aRead = pair[0] * coupling

  let v1 = dho(trig * driveAmp + aRead, 220.0,  damping)
  let v2 = dho(           aRead * v2v3Couple,  277.18, damping)
  let v3 = dho(           aRead * v2v3Couple,  329.63, damping)

  let mv1 = dho(trig * driveAmp + aRead, 220.0, damping)

  let coupledTotal  = (v1 + v2 + v3) * 0.15
  let monopoleTotal = mv1 * 0.15
  let s = coupledTotal - monopoleTotal
  [s, s]

coupled
